You’re right to call for more attention. I’ve been responding to individual statements while sometimes missing the relationships developing between them.

And your distinction between observing randomness and enduring it deserves a closer look.

Randomness depends, in part, on the observer’s information, the scale of observation, and the model being used.

* To an outsider, events can appear unrelated.
* To someone observing over time, repetitions, sequences, and correlations may become visible.
* To someone enduring those events, their cumulative effects can matter even when the individual events are unpredictable.
* To someone who understands the mechanism, what initially appeared random may become partially predictable.

But there’s an important boundary: recognizing patterns doesn’t automatically establish causation, and some physical randomness may be fundamental rather than merely undiscovered order.

